  "notes": "Built from the archived 2022-23 bulletin (bulletins.psu.edu/archive/2022-2023/undergraduate/colleges/eberly-science/chemistry-bs/), fetched and diffed directly against the 2023-24 archive edition: the two are byte-identical for every course/credit/requirement (only cosmetic Gen-Ed-policy boilerplate wording and the fourth concentration tab's name changing from '2 + 2 Option' to 'Chemistry, B.S. at Commonwealth Campuses' differ) -- CHEM-2023.json is modeled identically to this file. Real structural differences from the 2026 edition, confirmed by fetching and directly comparing the archived Program Requirements and Suggested Academic Plan tables: (1) CHEM 316 ('The Professional Chemist') was a 1-credit course this era, not 2 credits (bumped to 2cr starting with the 2025-26 edition -- see CHEM-2025.json/CHEM-2026.json); (2) PHYS 213 (Fluids and Thermal Physics, 2cr) and PHYS 214 (Wave Motion and Quantum Physics, 2cr) were both separately Prescribed Courses this era -- 4cr of physics beyond PHYS 211/212 that the 2025-26 overhaul dropped entirely from the requirement list -- added here as explicit Semester 4 courses; (3) the elective pool was 16 credits of 400-level chemistry (not 15) and 17 credits of Supporting Courses (not 13), both larger than the current edition; (4) the degree's overall minimum was 125 credits (94 major + 45 Gen Ed, 15cr overlapping), not 2026's 120 (86 major + 45 Gen Ed, 14cr overlapping); (5) the four concentration options were named 'Analytical', 'Physical', 'Synthetic/Biological', and a 2+2 Commonwealth Campuses track -- different labels from 2026's 'Analytical/Environmental-Focused, Inorganic/Materials-Focused, Organic/Medicinal-Focused, Physical/Computational-Focused' -- but the same generic-elective-pool modeling approach CHEM-2026.json's own notes already use (draw from one shared 400-level pool, model the first-listed option's own suggested sequence) applies equally well here, since all four concentrations share the identical Prescribed Courses list and only differ in which specific 400-level electives the suggested plan names for each specialization. Modeled here: the Analytical Concentration's own suggested academic plan (the first/default tab on the archived page). The generic 400-Level CHEM elective pool is modeled as 5 slots summing to 16cr (matching this era's real floor exactly); the Supporting Course pool is modeled as 5 slots summing to 15cr (2cr under the real 17cr floor -- the archived Suggested Academic Plan's own visible 'Supporting course' line items also sum to exactly 15cr, so this is not an arbitrary shortfall but matches what the bulletin's own suggested plan actually shows, same 'slightly under' convention CHEM-2026.json already uses for its own Supporting Course pool). Same post-prereq-parser-fix repair as CHEM-2026.json: CHEM 110's real prereq chains through MATH 22 <- MATH 21 <- MATH 4 <- MATH 3, none of which the archived plan lists explicitly (a scraped-catalog artifact independent of catalog year, confirmed present for this era too) -- added the same explicit MATH 3/4 (Semester 1) and MATH 21/22 (Semester 2) chain used in CHEM-2026.json. Slot credits for GEN ED/electives are approximations of the printed plan's own per-semester totals and can be edited freely, same convention as CMPSC-2026.json's own notes.",
